        {
          "role": "assistant",
          "text": "RISK LEVEL: HIGH\n\nWHY:\n- Asks for an OTP, which allows scammers to breach and hijack your accounts.\n- Uses a fake car prize lure to steal sensitive verification credentials.\n\nRECOMMENDATION:\n- Never share OTPs or security codes with anyone under any circumstances.\n- Block the requester immediately and ignore offers promising prizes for credentials.",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-04T08:33:43.526Z",
          "messageType": "analysis"
        },
        {
          "role": "user",
          "text": "why when people want to get my otp and it's a scam",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-04T08:35:29.801Z"
        },
        {
          "role": "assistant",
          "text": "An OTP (One-Time Password) acts as the final security key to your personal bank account, emails, or online profiles. When someone asks you to share your OTP, it is almost always a scam because:\n\n1. **It gives them full access:** Sharing an OTP allows a scammer to authorize bank transfers, reset your passwords, or take over your accounts entirely.\n2. **Real organizations never ask for it:** Legitimate banks, companies, and official services will never contact you asking for an OTP. It is strictly meant for your private input on official screens only.\n3. **Lures are used as bait:** Scammers invent exciting prizes (like winning a car) or urgent warnings (like a blocked account) to distract you so you hand over the code without thinking twice.\n\nAlways treat your OTP like the private PIN to your ATM card or the physical key to your home—never give it to anyone who asks for it.",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-04T08:35:29.803Z",
          "messageType": "follow_up"
        },
        {
          "role": "user",
          "text": "any suggestions for me to do next time",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-04T09:02:40.925Z"
        },
        {
          "role": "assistant",
          "text": "Next time you encounter suspicious messages, follow these key practices:\n\n1. **Never share OTPs, passwords, or bank details** with anyone, no matter the reason.\n2. **Verify directly** through official websites or official phone numbers, never using links in unsolicited messages.\n3. **Treat high urgency, free prizes, or unexpected money offers** as immediate red flags.",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-04T09:02:40.927Z",
          "messageType": "follow_up"
