          "text": "so if its company and not companyy, so is it real or scam?",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-04T08:27:44.138Z"
        },
        {
          "role": "assistant",
          "text": "Not necessarily. Even if the domain is spelled correctly (`company.com`), it does not automatically guarantee the job offer is real. \n\nScammers can still use legitimate company names to carry out scams through several methods:\n\n1. **Email Spoofing:** Technical tricks can sometimes make an email appear to come from a legitimate domain when it actually did not.\n2. **Fake Recruitment Processes:** Scammers may pretend to be HR representatives of a real company, conduct quick text-based \"interviews,\" and later ask you to pay for work equipment, software, or background checks.\n3. **Unsolicited Outreach:** Legitimate companies rarely hire via random unsolicited emails without a formal application process first.\n\n**How to verify safely:**\n- Search for the company's official website independently (do not click links in the email).\n- Check their official \"Careers\" page to see if the job position is actually listed.\n- Contact the company's official HR or recruiter directly through verified channels (like their official LinkedIn page).\n- Remember: Real employers will **never** ask you to pay money for equipment, training, or job processing.",
